For $w,v \in H^2(K)$ for all $K \in \mathcal{T}_h$ we have
\begin{multline*}
A(w+v,\phi)-A(w,\phi)= -\sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_h}  \int_{K} \big(\det D^2(w+v)-\det D^2w\big)\phi\,d x \\+ 
\sum_{e \in \mathcal{E}_h^i } \int_e \[\{\!\{\cof D^2 (w+v) \}\!\} D (w+v) \] \phi \,d s - \sum_{e \in \mathcal{E}_h^i } \int_e \[\{\!\{\cof D^2 w \}\!\} D w \] \phi
\,d s,
\end{multline*}
for all $\phi \in H^1_0(\Omega)$.
Since $D^2 w$ is a $2 \times 2$ matrix, $\det D^2(w+v)= \det D^2 w + \det D^2 v +\cof D^2 w : D^2 v$. 

Thus 
\begin{multline*}
A(w+v,\phi)-A(w,\phi)= -\sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_h}  \int_{K} (\det D^2v)\phi\,d x  -\sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_h}  \int_{K} (\cof D^2 w: D^2v)\phi\,d x \\+ \sum_{e \in \mathcal{E}_h^i } \int_e \[\{\!\{\cof D^2 w \}\!\} D v \] \phi\,d s + \sum_{e \in \mathcal{E}_h^i } \int_e \[\{\!\{\cof D^2 v \}\!\} D w \] \phi\,d s.
+ \sum_{e \in \mathcal{E}_h^i } \int_e \[\{\!\{\cof D^2 v \}\!\} D v \] \phi\,d s.
\end{multline*}
By \eqref{det2} $\cof  D^2 w: D^2 v = \operatorname{div}\big((\cof D^2 w) D v )$.
This implies that
\begin{align*} 
A'(w;v,\phi) & = -\sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_h} \int_{K} \operatorname{div} \big((\cof  D^2 w) D v\big) \phi \,d x +
\sum_{e \in \mathcal{E}_h^i } \int_e \[\{\!\{\cof D^2 w \}\!\} D v \] \phi 
\,d s \\ & \qquad \qquad \quad + \sum_{e \in \mathcal{E}_h^i } \int_e \[\{\!\{\cof D^2 v \}\!\} D w \] \phi
\,d s,
\end{align*}
and $R(w;v,\phi)$ is given by \eqref{rdef1}.



By integration by parts and using the fact $\phi=0$ on $\partial \Omega$,
